title={Model-oriented web services},
journal={Proceedings - 2016 IEEE Symposium on Service-Oriented System Engineering, SOSE 2016},
year={2016},
pages={14-23},
doi={10.1109/SOSE.2016.64},
art_number={7473005},
note={cited By 3; Conference of IEEE Symposium on Service-Oriented System Engineering, SOSE 2016 ; Conference Date: 29 March 2016 Through 1 April 2016;  Conference Code:121727},
url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84979073970&doi=10.1109%2fSOSE.2016.64&partnerID=40&md5=954ce7935035851bf6ba53cbf9e73f55},
abstract={Model-Driven Software Engineering (MDSE) has been successfully applied in order to accelerate the development of Web Services (WS). Despite the reported successes identified by a systematic mapping in this context, there was no reported method that takes advantage of modelling facilities outside the development phases by exposing the models as a feature to the end-users. Therefore, in this paper, we study how would be possible to push MDSE to be more than a method to accelerate development. In our envision, MDSE could be evolved into a paradigm suitable to create WS systems that present modelling principles from MDSE as features visible to the end-users while functioning employing tools originally meant for MDSE development for several other uses. We refer to these MDSE-evolved WS systems as Model Oriented Web Services (MOWS). We also provide a pattern and a development process to guide the development of MOWS. This paper includes case studies that indicate evidences on its feasibility. We also discuss advantages, disadvantages and suitability for real world applications. © 2016 IEEE.},
